Path traversal is also known as directory traversal.
These vulnerabilities enable an attacker to read arbitrary files on the server that is running an application. 
This might include:
- Application code and data.
- Credentials for back-end systems.
- Sensitive operating system files

FOR EXAMPLE:
Imagine an online store uses a URL like ://example.com to let customers download their receipts from a folder on the server called /var/www/invoices/.
Behind the scenes, the application simply takes whatever text is placed after file= and attaches it straight to that folder path without checking if it is safe. 
Because the app blindly trusts the input, an attacker can swap invoice.pdf for ../../../etc/passwd (or ../../../app/.env).
Those ../ sequences instruct the server's operating system to step backward out of the invoices folder into restricted system directories, 
tricking the website into returning sensitive passwords, configuration keys, or source code instead of a receipt.
